WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.180
If you believe that Russia's invasion of Ukraine&nbsp;
is going to be the only one of the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.880
you may be right... But... in&nbsp;
order to make such a forecast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.320
with confidence would require us to know&nbsp;
for sure how China is going to behave.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.620
You already know that this country has&nbsp;
been threatening to invade Taiwan for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.620
some time now. And, I’m sure many of&nbsp;
you would think that sooner or later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.580
the island will likely become the next&nbsp;
Ukraine, but... What if we told you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.860
there are those who think that ultimately the&nbsp;
Asian Ukraine could be Vietnam and not Taiwan?

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.380
And, keep in mind, we are not&nbsp;
talking about mere speculation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.340
There are plenty of arguments&nbsp;
to believe that the risk exists.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.380
Tension rises between China, Vietnam and&nbsp;
the Philippines over disputed islands

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.940
In fact, China has even sunk Vietnamese&nbsp;
fishing boats in the South China Sea. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.260
let's be honest, this is nothing new for Vietnam,&nbsp;
after all, the two countries were already at war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:58.080
in 1979, just after the end of the famous civil&nbsp;
war in which the United States intervened.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.000
[But, wait a minute, aren't they both supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.980
to be countries with communist regimes?&nbsp;
Didn't international socialism unite them?)

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.500
Yes, both were – and supposedly still&nbsp;
are – staunch communist regimes, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.740
when it comes to power struggles...&nbsp;
ideology takes a back seat.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.060
The fact is that it should come as no surprise&nbsp;
that Vietnam is trying to strengthen itself as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.720
much as possible to avoid another possible&nbsp;
Chinese invasion. And what can I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.800
history tells us that this is the smartest&nbsp;
move it can make. And, not surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.060
this is something that, as we will see,&nbsp;
is of great interest to the United States.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.900
Think about it, the United States wants to curb&nbsp;
Chinese expansionism in the South China Sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.820
an objective shared by Vietnam, a country that&nbsp;
could play a crucial role in that project.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.860
With almost 100 million inhabitants and impressive&nbsp;
economic growth in recent years – Vietnam is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.880
gradually emerging as another future regional&nbsp;
counterweight to China. One more. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.900
alongside Japan, South Korea, Singapore, the&nbsp;
Philippines, possibly India and so many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.000
countries in the area. China's position is not&nbsp;
as strong as it might appear at first glance.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.260
However, we must not forget that Vietnam&nbsp;
is still a communist dictatorship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.340
and making alliances with authoritarian&nbsp;
regimes can lead to a lot of problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.720
Just look at the NATO situation with Turkey...&nbsp;
But we'll talk about that in another video.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.380
For now, the question is: can the&nbsp;
West really trust Vietnam? To what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.960
extent could it become a reliable&nbsp;
military partner for the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.100
States? Could tensions between Vietnam&nbsp;
and China end up provoking another war?

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:35.280
Today we are going to answer these questions,&nbsp;
but first let's look at a bit of history.&nbsp;

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.220
(THE CROSSROADS OF ASIAN COMMUNISM)
 &nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.900
When we look at history and how China&nbsp;
and Vietnam are organized internally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.920
we see that, at first glance, they&nbsp;
basically appear to be parallel realities:

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.200
Both countries carried out socialist revolutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.040
both established communist dictatorships&nbsp;
that are still in place, and both began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.760
to reform their economies in order to&nbsp;
open up to the international market.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.720
Vietnam started its economic&nbsp;
reform in 1986 with Doi Moi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.680
that is 8 years after Deng Xiaoping's&nbsp;
economic reforms were launched in China.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.360
The fact is that both countries began to promote&nbsp;
private ownership of agricultural property and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.720
in general, in the rest of the economy.&nbsp;
They also boosted foreign investment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.240
intensified their trade relations with the world.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.000
Thanks to these changes, they&nbsp;
eventually reaped a juicy result:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.060
as you can see, their economies began to take off.&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.840
As you can see, China, which began&nbsp;
its reforms almost a decade earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.160
distanced itself from Vietnam, especially when&nbsp;
it joined the World Trade Organization at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.360
beginning of the 21st century. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
overall, the two countries have followed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.260
more than remarkable trajectory. In just 30&nbsp;
years, Vietnam has multiplied the real value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.120
of its production by 12 times, while China&nbsp;
has increased its own by almost 20 times.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.900
And the truth is that we are talking about two&nbsp;
countries with many similarities... But that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.300
at the same time, are also very different.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.620
In the case of Vietnam, in addition to arriving&nbsp;
almost a decade late to the wave of economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.820
reforms, it must be taken into account that&nbsp;
when it did finally arrive, this country had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:14.040
just gone through two wars: a civil war in&nbsp;
which the United States participated and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.880
later, a war against China, which showed&nbsp;
up in defense of the Cambodian regime that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.320
committed one of the most extensive&nbsp;
acts of genocide in history, Pol Pot.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.340
In general, we can say that Vietnam embraced&nbsp;
reforms when it was poorer and had even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.180
problems of all kinds than China. In part,&nbsp;
that difference explains the current gap.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.380
Of course, today, the differences&nbsp;
go beyond the purely economic realm.&nbsp;

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.180
(China, between collective power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.560
and the personalism of Xi Jinping. – ABC)
 
Exactly, while in recent years personalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.800
taken to the extreme has regained&nbsp;
strength with Xi Jinping in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.660
in Vietnam things look different, and&nbsp;
proof of this is news stories like this:&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:53.700
(Vietnam President Nguyen Xuan Phuc quits amid&nbsp;
Communist Party corruption crackdown. – CNN)

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:58.320
You see, in China, since 1989, the general&nbsp;
secretary of the Communist Party has also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.880
been the president of the country. In Vietnam,&nbsp;
on the other hand, this seems unthinkable today.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.780
In fact, the Vietnamese communist&nbsp;
dictatorship rests on four pillars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.200
the General Secretariat, the Prime Minister,&nbsp;
the President and the Leader of the National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.100
Assembly. Since Ho Chi Minh's death in&nbsp;
1969, each of these positions has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.300
held by different people who function, in&nbsp;
a way, as counterweights to each other.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.020
In fact, the National Assembly of Vietnam,&nbsp;
which is obviously not democratic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.040
at least has some real activity. It can&nbsp;
even veto resolutions and, if necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.820
propose motions of confidence against the&nbsp;
prime minister. So far this has allowed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.340
official apparatus to be somewhat more oxygenated.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, in China there was the alternation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.560
the leader and the political elites... But&nbsp;
with Xi Jinping, all that has disappeared.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:48.120
So what does all this mean? Basically, no&nbsp;
one in Vietnam can change course rapidly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:53.160
as Xi Jinping can in China. And it also generally&nbsp;
means fewer restrictions for the population.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.780
For example, while in China Facebook and YouTube&nbsp;
are totally blocked, in Vietnam there is free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.740
access, albeit with some restrictions, even&nbsp;
to videos that criticize the Communist Party.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.400
But apart from the domestic political project,&nbsp;
the area where we find the biggest differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.020
between the two countries is mainly in their&nbsp;
policy in the South China Sea, although, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.280
they also have one important thing in common&nbsp;
there: both countries claim the same territories.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.000
And that means that tensions could flare&nbsp;
up and history could repeat itself again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.200
Don't forget that both countries&nbsp;
were at war as recently as 1979.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.000
The question is: How does Vietnam hope to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.640
prevent tension from flaring&nbsp;
up with its northern neighbor?

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:33.660
Well, let's take a look.
(FROM RUSSIA, WITH LOVE)

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:41.400
It is inevitable, every time we hear about&nbsp;
Vietnam, we immediately think of that war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.220
about which dozens of movies have been&nbsp;
made and in which the United States was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.700
involved in supporting the south of the&nbsp;
country with tens and tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:52.680
of soldiers. To give you an idea, in 1968&nbsp;
the North American power had almost 600,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.620
soldiers deployed in this country. In&nbsp;
total, the United States participated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.280
in this war for 12 very long years.
But I'm sure what many of you didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.080
know is that right after this war&nbsp;
ended, China invaded Vietnam in 1979.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.060
And apparently today things&nbsp;
would not be much different:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.120
Vietnam's exposure to China remains enormous.&nbsp;

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:16.980
Look at this map: If you pay attention,&nbsp;
geographically Vietnam is totally at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.800
mercy of China, either by its border in&nbsp;
the north – which was where the Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:23.820
invaded them in '79 – or by the coast where,&nbsp;
in addition to being practically surrounded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.480
they would face the largest navy by&nbsp;
number of ships in the whole world.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.080
And let's not forget that to the southwest&nbsp;
there is Cambodia, an ally of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.560
and then there is Laos, which could change&nbsp;
sides at the convenience of the highest bidder.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.360
Not surprisingly, faced with such a scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:40.860
the question is, can Vietnam really&nbsp;
have a clearly independent discourse?

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.340
This is basically the reason why Vietnam&nbsp;
has been moving closer and closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.300
countries such as Japan and India in recent&nbsp;
years, both militarily and commercially,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.020
But, let's be honest, the friendship between&nbsp;
these countries is rather superficial. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.440
we really want to identify the one who&nbsp;
has always been Vietnam's best friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:00.000
we have to go to the country that has been in all&nbsp;
the headlines around the world this past year.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.280
Exactly, we are talking about Russia.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:12.060
Since the Ho Chi Minh era, Moscow's influence&nbsp;
in the country has already been infinitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.240
greater than that of Beijing. In a way,&nbsp;
this has much to do with the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.840
the roots of Vietnam's communist thinking&nbsp;
come directly from Soviet Marxism-Leninism.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.920
It is not surprising, therefore, that its ties&nbsp;
with the Soviet Union were so close. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.700
explains, to a large extent, why the United&nbsp;
States took the Vietnam War so seriously. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:30.480
conflict in which North Vietnamese troops were&nbsp;
supported and assisted by the Soviet Union.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.220
Which is exactly what happened again when China&nbsp;
invaded the country in 1979 in retaliation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.700
Vietnam attacking and overthrowing the&nbsp;
bloody Khmer Rouge regime in Cambodia.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.000
Whether the invasion was coming from the&nbsp;
Americans or the Chinese, the Soviet Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.560
the most loyal partner of communist Vietnam,&nbsp;
helped the country by sending a lot of weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.600
such as anti-aircraft missiles, light machine&nbsp;
guns and the infamous AK-47 assault rifle.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.820
The point is that if their&nbsp;
relations were already close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:01.380
from that era on the friendship grew much&nbsp;
stronger. In fact, today Russia remains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.820
largest arms partner of the Vietnamese army.&nbsp;
Russia even has preferential access to one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.180
of the most important Vietnamese&nbsp;
bases, the one in Cam Ranh Bay.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.460
But pay attention, because&nbsp;
that's not the end of it.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.440
Vietnam did not rely on the Soviet&nbsp;
Union and today's Russia just to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.740
weapons. Economic cooperation has&nbsp;
also been key during this time.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.760
Do you want an example? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.120
in recent years Vietnam has tried to&nbsp;
extract oil in the South China Sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.900
with the help of a lot of multinationals. For&nbsp;
example, with the Spanish company, Repsol.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:35.520
However, we are talking about extraction from&nbsp;
disputed territories, and, naturally, the oil was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.780
going to come out faster and with fewer problems&nbsp;
if some Russian state-owned company got involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.340
and managed to intimidate China a little bit, just&nbsp;
a little bit. [Particularly for fear of damaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:49.020
its relations with Moscow. Or rather, Xi Jinping's&nbsp;
determination to maintain those relations].

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.440
And, of course, the first to jump on this&nbsp;
opportunity was Rosneft, although it later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.240
sold its entire stake to Zarubezhneft,&nbsp;
another Russian state-owned energy company.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:03.180
So let me ask you a question, how do you&nbsp;
think Vietnam repays Russia's favors? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:06.000
news stories like this are&nbsp;
a pretty good indication:&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:11.850
(Vietnam and Thailand abstain from UN vote&nbsp;
to condemn Russia's Ukraine annexation. RFA)

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.860
Exactly. Vietnam is one of the few countries in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.740
the world that avoids condemning&nbsp;
the Russian invasion of Ukraine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:24.240
And, it makes sense, if it weren't for the&nbsp;
constant support of the Soviet Union in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.080
past and the current Russia today, Vietnam&nbsp;
would possibly not be what it is today.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.860
But of course, if China were to take the&nbsp;
initiative and punish or change the Vietnamese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.180
government, or even invade the country and turn&nbsp;
it into a satellite State, the Russians would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.920
be of no help. Today, given the new capabilities&nbsp;
of the Chinese army and the manifest weaknesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.900
of Russia, it would be very difficult, if not&nbsp;
impossible, for Moscow to be of any help to them.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:50.040
And make no mistake about it. This is crystal&nbsp;
clear to the decision makers in Hanoi:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.580
they have one of the world's major powers on&nbsp;
their doorstep, a power that is increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.940
expansionist and with which they do&nbsp;
not get along particularly well. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:59.220
in any case, they do not want&nbsp;
to be a satellite country.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:07.440
To avoid a possible clash, in recent years,&nbsp;
Hanoi has been trying to improve its relations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.220
with Beijing. This is not just a territorial&nbsp;
or military issue. To put it in perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.900
almost 40% of all Vietnamese imports come from&nbsp;
China, including integrated circuits, telephones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:20.220
semiconductors... In short, practically everything&nbsp;
the country needs to develop technologically.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:25.980
This explains, for example, why both&nbsp;
countries have carried out dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.500
military cooperation exercises&nbsp;
during the current century.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:33.000
And above all, it explains the foreign&nbsp;
policy based on the philosophy of the 3 noes:&nbsp;

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.080
Not to allow foreign military bases in Vietnam,&nbsp;
not to be part of military alliances, and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:42.300
to support one country in its fight against&nbsp;
another. All this in order not to anger China.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:45.540
But what if this strategy&nbsp;
was a mistake? Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.660
tensions in the South China Sea with&nbsp;
China have been rising over the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:52.260
decade and the situation in the region is&nbsp;
no longer to be taken lightly. Watch this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.100
space:(LEAVING THE PAST BEHIND)

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:07.260
We've told you before how Vietnam embarked&nbsp;
on a series of pro-market economic reforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:11.880
starting in '86. It's something we talked about&nbsp;
in detail in a past video here on VisualPolitik.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.640
But for those of you who haven't&nbsp;
seen it or simply don't remember it:

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.000
At the beginning, these reforms were&nbsp;
limited to the agricultural sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.660
and later jumped to the rest of the economic&nbsp;
sectors. One of the most important was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:25.680
pursuit of the country's industrialization&nbsp;
at all costs. In order to succeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.740
this required receiving and courting&nbsp;
investors from the rest of the world.

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.020
And, trust me, they took it very seriously.
Nowadays it is not uncommon to see brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:38.340
from all over the world producing in Vietnam. We&nbsp;
are talking about companies like Samsung, H&amp;M,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:43.740
Adidas, Nike and Foxconn. In fact, Vietnam's&nbsp;
largest customer by far is the United States.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.300
This is not surprising. Relations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.660
the North American superpower have&nbsp;
improved greatly in recent years.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:58.320
Gone are the times of war and mistrust. In 1995&nbsp;
the two countries formally restored diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:03.300
relations, in 2000 they signed a Bilateral Trade&nbsp;
Agreement, in 2007 Vietnam joined the World Trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:08.100
Organization with Washington's explicit support,&nbsp;
and in 2016 the United States completely lifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:12.660
the arms embargo on Vietnam, allowing for greater&nbsp;
cooperation on security and defense issues.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.420
We could even see images like these:&nbsp;

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:21.540
Since then, the two countries have conducted&nbsp;
numerous joint military exercises and routinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:25.980
collaborate on maritime security and freedom&nbsp;
of navigation in the Indo-Pacific region.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:28.380
Now, while we have told you that&nbsp;
Vietnam has tried to get along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.740
as well as possible with China, what&nbsp;
explains its sudden desire to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.940
a link with its historical archenemy&nbsp;
and China's current great adversary?

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:47.220
Well, beyond economic issues, the truth is that in&nbsp;
Hanoi they do not trust Beijing at all. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:51.120
in 2014, tension between the two countries&nbsp;
reached an extreme level when China brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:56.160
a semi-submersible oil platform to explore and&nbsp;
exploit oil within Vietnamese maritime territory.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.520
And not only that, the construction of&nbsp;
artificial islands around the Spratly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:04.020
Paracel archipelagos, which Vietnam claims as its&nbsp;
own, has caused tension to grow in the region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:07.680
especially because of the military hardware&nbsp;
that China is installing on these islands.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:10.680
That is why in Hanoi they now seem&nbsp;
to understand that neither Moscow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:13.680
nor Beijing are their safest bets,&nbsp;
but that instead they need to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.620
to the United States and its orbit&nbsp;
of allied countries in the area.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:20.220
Of course, Washington has seen this shift&nbsp;
as a stroke of good fortune: as another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:24.720
potential partner to add to its already long&nbsp;
list of countries angry or suspicious of China.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:28.080
And in a way that's what&nbsp;
explains news items like these:

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:35.160
(29 March 2018: The United States Transfers Six&nbsp;
Metal Shark Patrol Boats to Vietnam. US Embassy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:40.440
Vietnam)(20 November 2019: US gifts&nbsp;
ship to Vietnam to boost patrols in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:45.540
disputed South China Sea. – SCMP)
(17 December 2022: Analysis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:49.800
Vietnam shifts gears on arms trade as&nbsp;
it loosens ties with Russia. – Reuters)

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:51.960
Now, instead of buying Russian weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.580
it is American, South Korean and Israeli&nbsp;
weapons that are flooding into Vietnam.

00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:59.760
Now, does this mean that Vietnam&nbsp;
is going to change its "3 noes"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:04.740
philosophy and throw itself completely into&nbsp;
the arms of Uncle Sam? Well, definitely not.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:12.720
Vietnam is a strange case&nbsp;
of a country that right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.220
seems to want to get along with Beijing,&nbsp;
Moscow and Washington at the same time.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:21.660
And make no mistake: Vietnam is still a communist&nbsp;
dictatorship. Western political and democratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:25.800
values are not part of the Communist Party,&nbsp;
and this limits the confidence that the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:30.000
States places in Vietnam. Not surprisingly, it&nbsp;
is practically impossible to imagine Vietnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.440
intervening alongside the United States&nbsp;
in Taiwan to prevent an attack by China.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:39.000
But now the question is for you, if you&nbsp;
were Vietnam, who would you side with:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.780
Moscow, Beijing or Washington? Leave&nbsp;
us your answers in the comments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:45.480
let's open a debate. If you&nbsp;
found this video interesting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:49.200
don't forget to like and subscribe to&nbsp;
VisualPolitik if you haven't already done so.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.400
Thank you very much for watching,

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.920
All the best and see you next time.

